          "In an imagined design office, an assistant can retrieve a past decision before a colleague has finished asking about it. It searches meeting notes, drafts and messages, then produces a fluent account of why a project changed direction. The service is popular with new employees, who once depended on finding the right person during a busy afternoon. To a manager, the system appears to turn scattered experience into an organizational memory. The trouble begins with the phrase \"why a project changed\": surviving documents often explain what was approved more clearly than what made other possibilities unacceptable.",
